    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This contribution is part of an on-going effort to improve the statistical characterization of fault and fracture patterns formed by a sheared joint mechanism, based on field work in the Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada. We first investigate the relationships between splays and parent faults. Our results suggest that maximum splay length is correlated to parent fault length/slip by power laws. Second, we study the influence of fault configuration (isolated and interacting faults) on kink angle between faults and splays. We observe that average kink angles are small (~20°) for isolated situations and large (~50°) when faults interact. Combining these two data sets may provide a criterion for the linkage of neighboring faults. Third, the characteristics of secondary fractures associated with faults are studied along scanlines across two left-lateral faults with 8m and 14m slip. The data from the two faults are very similar, and are compared with previous results obtained from large- scale faults.\r\n\r\nThe ultimate goal of this project is to build a geostatistical model accounting for the geometry and relationships between faults and fractures at different scales in the Valley of Fire, with a direct application to flow simulation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
